<h2 id="sec-1">The Verified Weekly Price</h2>
<p>The verified weekly rate for the Yamaha Sirius in Hanoi is 700,000 VND for a full week on the same machine. That figure comes straight from the shop's published prices, and this guide will not invent anything beyond it: no discounts, no promotions, no invented bundle. What you see on the price list is the planning number.</p>
<p>Divide it and the week costs 100,000 VND per day, against 150,000 VND per day on the daily rate. The saving is 350,000 VND over seven days compared with paying daily, which is why the weekly rate is the default structure for any stay that touches five days or more.</p>
<p>The price sits in the entry tier of the fleet. The Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click share the same verified structure, 150,000 VND per day and 700,000 VND per week, so choosing within the entry tier is a choice of machine, not of price. The step-up tier, the Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade and the electric motorbike, rents at 200,000 VND per day and 1,000,000 VND per week.</p>
<p>A weekly rental is also a commitment decision. The week is booked on one machine for seven days, so the plan should be honest about how many of those days the scooter will actually roll. A week with four riding days still beats four daily rates, but only barely, and that math is worth doing before the counter.</p>
<h2 id="sec-2">What the Sirius Buys for a Week</h2>
<p>The Yamaha Sirius is a lightweight semi-automatic underbone, a machine built around simplicity. A week of Hanoi riding is exactly its job description: city lanes, short hops, Old Quarter runs and the daily commute between a hotel and the places worth seeing. Its light weight makes it easy to park in the tight rows that pass for parking in Hanoi, and the upright riding position keeps a full day of stop-and-go traffic comfortable.</p>
<p>The Sirius is also frugal, which matters on a weekly rental more than a daily one. Fuel over seven days is a real line in the budget, and a machine that sips keeps that line small. Confirm the fuel arrangement at handover, full out and full back is the common pattern, and budget a modest weekly figure for city distances.</p>
<p>For riders, the honest summary is that the Sirius is transport, not spectacle. It will not impress anyone at a cafe, and it is not the machine for long highway distances. For a week of city living it is the correct answer, and the entry-tier weekly price is the cheapest verified way to live that week on two wheels.</p>
<h2 id="sec-3">Planning a Weekly Budget</h2>
<p>Plan the week from three numbers: the rental itself, fuel, and the small daily costs that come with riding. The rental is fixed at 700,000 VND for the week, the one line that cannot move. Fuel depends on distance, and a city week of ordinary riding keeps it modest on the Sirius. Parking, where paid lots are used, is typically a few thousand VND per stop.</p>
<p>Set the week around real plans. A typical tourist week in Hanoi uses the scooter for Old Quarter days, a lake loop, a market run, and one or two trips across the river to Long Bien. Each of those is a short ride, and the Sirius carries a week of them without complaint. Riders planning bigger loops should confirm the route with the desk, since a weekly rental on an entry-tier machine is a city tool first.</p>
<p>Keep a day un-ridden deliberately. A rainy Hanoi afternoon happens, and the weekly rate means a parked day costs nothing extra. This is the quiet advantage of the week over the day: the day rate punishes a rest day at full price, while the week absorbs it at roughly 14,000 VND of unused daily value, a number close enough to zero to ignore.</p>
<p>The budgeting mistake is planning the week as seven full riding days. Plan five or six honest days, let the weather or a walking day take the rest, and the weekly rate still wins comfortably. A budget with slack in it is the one that survives contact with a real week in Hanoi.</p>
<h2 id="sec-4">The Daily Rate Comparison</h2>
<p>The daily rate for the Sirius is 150,000 VND, so five daily rates already total 750,000 VND against the 700,000 VND week. The break-even is at five days: any stay of five days or longer should be booked weekly, and the counter will convert an ongoing daily rental on request. Ask, do not assume, because conversion terms belong to the desk.</p>
<p>Below five days the daily rate stays honest. A two or three day city stay pays 300,000 to 450,000 VND at the daily rate, well under the week, so the week is not a default. The two structures are tools for different plans, and the honest planner picks the one that matches the calendar rather than the one that sounds cheaper per day.</p>
<p>The comparison mistake is extending daily past the fifth day. Riders do it out of inertia, and the arithmetic quietly costs 50,000 VND per day. Mark the fifth day on the calendar as decision day, and the week takes over at exactly the right moment.</p>
<h2 id="sec-5">The Monthly Rate Comparison</h2>
<p>The monthly rate for the Sirius sits in a verified range of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND per month, and the exact figure inside that range depends on the machine and terms, so confirm it with the desk for your dates rather than estimating. Even at the top of the range, a month costs less than two weeks booked back to back, which is a dramatic saving for long stays.</p>
<p>The break-even between weekly and monthly is early: roughly nine to ten days of weekly rentals already approach the monthly figure. Stays of ten days or more should ask about the monthly rate before paying for a second week. The counter can run the numbers for the exact dates in a minute.</p>
<p>The honest boundary is the calendar. Between five and nine days, the week is the right structure. Below five, the day. Beyond ten, ask about the month, and let the verified range, not a guess, settle it. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub puts every model against every period in one table.</p>
<h2 id="sec-6">Sirius Versus Other Weekly Rates</h2>
<p>Within the entry tier the weekly price is identical: Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio, Honda Click and Yamaha Sirius all rent at 700,000 VND per week. The choice inside the tier is purely about the machine, so ride whichever feels right at handover and do not pay attention to the price list, because it is not moving.</p>
<p>The step-up tier doubles the weekly premium. The Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade and the electric motorbike rent at 1,000,000 VND per week, which is 300,000 VND more than the Sirius for the same seven days. That premium buys a different kind of machine, a proper automatic scooter with more storage and smoother delivery for the petrol machines, or silent electric running for the EV.</p>
<p>There is also a 50cc option for riders who want the smallest engine class, at a verified 200,000 VND per day. For weekly terms on a 50cc machine, the desk confirms the figure for your dates rather than the page inventing one, which is how a verified-prices shop works.</p>
<p>The comparison mistake is upgrading without a reason. A week of city lanes does not need the step-up tier, and 300,000 VND buys several very good meals in Hanoi. Upgrade for comfort or storage reasons, not because the brochure in your head says so.</p>
<h2 id="sec-7">Deposits, Gear and Extras</h2>
<p>The weekly rate is the headline, not the whole transaction. Ask what the deposit is, in what form it is held, and what has to be true at return for it to come back in full. Cash deposits get receipts and card holds get release dates, and both questions take ten seconds each at the counter.</p>
<p>Ask what is included with the week: helmets for each rider, a lock, rain ponchos. Inclusion is a question, not an assumption, and confirming it at handover prevents a mid-week surprise. A week is long enough for rain in Hanoi, and a poncho in the under-seat storage is worth more than the space it takes.</p>
<p>Ask about support before it is needed: the number to call if the machine has a problem, and the swap procedure if a check fails during the week. A weekly rental is a relationship, not a transaction, and shops that rent by the week expect these questions.</p>
<p>The extras mistake is signing in a hurry. The weekly agreement is one page and takes two minutes to read, and every verbal answer about deposit, fuel and inclusions becomes a promise on that page. Read it in the shade before riding off.</p>
<h2 id="sec-8">When the Week Is the Wrong Rate</h2>
<p>The week is the wrong structure for stays of four days or fewer, where the daily rate totals less than 700,000 VND, and for stays of ten days or more, where the monthly range of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND should be negotiated instead. Between those boundaries, five to nine days, the week is exactly right, and the 100,000 VND per day it averages is the cheapest verified way to ride the Sirius in Hanoi.</p>
<p>It is also the wrong rate when the plan is uncertain. If the trip might shrink to three days or stretch to three weeks, say so at the counter, because a good desk would rather structure the rental correctly once than unwind it twice. Uncertainty is a planning input, not an embarrassment.</p>
<p>Our team at Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu is happy to run your dates against all three rates for the Yamaha Sirius, confirm availability, and hand over a machine that passes inspection. Plan the stay honestly, and the rate picks itself: 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week, or the monthly figure that makes a long stay cheap.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions about Yamaha Sirius weekly rental prices in Hanoi</h2>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How much does a Yamaha Sirius cost per week to rent in Hanoi?</h2>
<p>The verified weekly rate is 700,000 VND for a full week, which averages 100,000 VND per day. The same machine rents at 150,000 VND per day and 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND per month.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How many days before the Sirius weekly rate beats the daily rate?</h2>
<p>Five days. Five daily rates total 750,000 VND while the week costs 700,000 VND for the same machine, so any stay of five days or more should be booked weekly, saving 350,000 VND over a full seven days.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>Is the Yamaha Sirius weekly price the same as the Honda Wave and Yamaha Mio?</h2>
<p>Yes. The Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click all rent at the same verified 700,000 VND per week alongside the Sirius. The step-up tier, Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade and the electric motorbike, rents at 1,000,000 VND per week.</p>
</article>
